\details{
Redundancy is estimated with a sample grid rather than an exact polygon
overlay, because an exact pairwise overlay across many overlapping
catchments is expensive and the exact boundary of that overlay is not what
the map needs -- a representative per-polygon summary is. A regular point
grid (\code{grid_spacing_miles} apart, in EPSG:5070 meters) is laid over the
union of \code{catchments}' bounding box and \code{polygons}; each grid point counts
the number of \strong{distinct} \code{provider_id_var} values among the catchments
containing it (\code{0} if none); each target polygon's value is the \strong{median}
grid-point count among points falling inside it. Median, not mean, so one
corner with much higher or lower redundancy than the rest of a large,
irregularly-shaped polygon does not dominate its summary value.

A small polygon can be narrower than \code{grid_spacing_miles} and catch zero
grid points at all -- on Virginia's independent cities (Lexington, Fairfax
City, Manassas, Manassas Park, Colonial Heights, Covington, Poquoson,
Emporia, Bristol, Charlottesville; all under 20 square miles) this left 10
of 133 polygons with no sample at all, rendered as a blank gap in
\code{na.value} even though they are not actually missing data. Any polygon the
grid misses entirely is sampled once more directly, at
\code{\link[sf:st_point_on_surface]{sf::st_point_on_surface()}} (guaranteed to land inside the polygon, unlike
a bounding-box centroid, which can fall outside an irregular shape).

A redundancy count is classified, not coloured continuously: \code{0} --
nothing covers this area at all -- is as categorically different from \code{1}
as \code{1} is different from \code{5}, and a continuous \code{scale_fill_viridis_c()}
(the original implementation) puts \code{0} at the dark end of the same
gradient as every low-but-nonzero value, where it reads as merely "low
redundancy" rather than the "no option at all" finding it actually is.
\code{\link[=mysterymaps_jenks_zero_scale]{mysterymaps_jenks_zero_scale()}} with \code{digits = NULL} gives whole-number
legend labels appropriate to a count and puts \code{0} in its own class, so it
replaces the old \code{scale_fill_viridis_c()} plus a hand-written
integer-breaks helper.

\code{catchments} must contain exactly one row per \verb{(provider, band)} you want
counted. If \code{catchments} holds multiple drive-time bands per provider,
filter to a single band before calling this function -- passing all bands
at once would double-count the same provider at a point reachable within
more than one of their bands.
